const whyLessons = [{"id":"WHY01","region":"east","period":"Warring States period–221 BCE","title":"Why could Qin unify China?","summary":"Institutions that mobilized people and supplies for war combined with a long process of territorial expansion.","causes":[{"title":"State institutions","text":"Reforms including those of Shang Yang strengthened population registration, taxation and government based on military merit, increasing mobilization capacity."},{"title":"Agriculture and resources","text":"Agriculture in Guanzhong and the Sichuan region helped provide the revenue and food needed to maintain armies."},{"title":"War and diplomacy","text":"Conquest involved military force, diplomacy that disrupted rival coalitions, and successive campaigns."}],"trigger":"Late in the Warring States period, Qin defeated the other major states in succession and achieved unification in 221 BCE.","chain":["Stronger institutions and agriculture","Sustained mobilization of soldiers and supplies","Conquest and centralization"],"consequences":["Commanderies and counties, standardized writing, weights and measures supported government over a large territory.","Heavy burdens and harsh rule also provoked resistance: successful unification did not guarantee lasting stability."],"nuance":"Saying only that the First Emperor was talented leaves unexplained the state institutions built through successive reforms.","reflection":"Explain Qin unification using the three words institutions, resources and diplomacy.","questions":[{"id":300000,"topic":"WHY01-0","type":"choice","era":"Warring States period–221 BCE","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY01","q":"Which combination best explains Qin unification?","answer":"Mobilization systems, agriculture, warfare and diplomacy worked together","options":["Mobilization systems, agriculture, warfare and diplomacy worked together","Writing was standardized first, making warfare unnecessary","Commanderies and counties permanently guaranteed alliances among the states","Hereditary aristocratic privileges were strengthened and reform avoided"],"ex":"Consider both state mobilization and the way the conquests were conducted.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · Ancient China","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-1/pages/5-1-ancient-china"}]},{"id":300001,"topic":"WHY01-1","type":"choice","era":"Warring States period–221 BCE","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY01","q":"What matters when assessing Qin after unification?","answer":"The mobilization that helped unification also burdened the population","options":["The mobilization that helped unification also burdened the population","Rebellions ceased once the country was unified","Standardization abolished all taxation","Local government was entrusted entirely to independent lords"],"ex":"Consider both increased state capacity and its social costs.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · Ancient China","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-1/pages/5-1-ancient-china"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · Ancient China","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-1/pages/5-1-ancient-china"}]},{"id":"WHY02","region":"east","period":"Han China and Eurasian exchange","title":"Why did Silk Road exchanges expand?","summary":"Imperial diplomacy and security overlapped with the movement of merchants and monks, linking many different routes.","causes":[{"title":"State diplomacy","text":"The Han sought to counter the Xiongnu and develop relations with the Western Regions."},{"title":"Demand for trade","text":"Demand for silk, horses, aromatics and other goods supported relay trade across regions."},{"title":"People on the move","text":"Sogdian and other merchants, along with monks, carried religions and knowledge as well as goods."}],"trigger":"The missions of Zhang Qian and Han expansion into the Western Regions were among the developments that broadened existing exchanges.","chain":["Expanding diplomacy and trade","Growth of oasis and other relay centres","Movement of religions, technologies and cultures"],"consequences":["Buddhism, crafts and technologies spread across regions.","Disease and military conflict also had connections with these wider networks."],"nuance":"Think of land and sea routes and many intermediaries, rather than a single road travelled end to end by one merchant.","reflection":"Explain why activities begun for political purposes could also encourage cultural exchange.","questions":[{"id":300002,"topic":"WHY02-0","type":"choice","era":"Han China and Eurasian exchange","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY02","q":"Which description of the Silk Roads is appropriate?","answer":"Multiple routes and intermediaries linked regions","options":["Multiple routes and intermediaries linked regions","Han China and Rome jointly built a single national highway","Chinese merchants carried every shipment of silk directly to Rome","Commerce and religious movement were unrelated"],"ex":"Trading networks consisted of many stages and many participants.","refs":[{"label":"UNESCO · Silk Road exchanges","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/silk-roads/about-silk-roads"}]},{"id":300003,"topic":"WHY02-1","type":"choice","era":"Han China and Eurasian exchange","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY02","q":"How could diplomacy and warfare affect cultural exchange?","answer":"Transport links and bases also supported merchants and monks","options":["Transport links and bases also supported merchants and monks","Military expansion permanently eliminated all trade","Buddhism abolished every state border before it spread","State policy alone completed all cultural exchange"],"ex":"Look at the overlap of state policies and non-state movement.","refs":[{"label":"UNESCO · Silk Road exchanges","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/silk-roads/about-silk-roads"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"UNESCO · Silk Road exchanges","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/silk-roads/about-silk-roads"}]},{"id":"WHY03","region":"east","period":"Tang–Song China","title":"Why did commerce and cities grow under the Song?","summary":"Rising production, improved distribution and urban demand reinforced one another.","causes":[{"title":"Agricultural development","text":"Southern development and improvements in rice cultivation expanded the food base for a growing population and non-farming workers."},{"title":"Transport and markets","text":"Canals, rivers and maritime shipping linked regional products and widened trade."},{"title":"Money and demand","text":"Increasing monetary exchange and urban consumption supported merchants and artisans."}],"trigger":"Building on changes since the late Tang, interregional trade and urban commerce became more active under the Song.","chain":["Rising production and population","Expanding distribution and markets","Urban consumption and commercial and craft growth"],"consequences":["Urban life and commercial culture flourished in cities such as Kaifeng and Hangzhou.","Institutions including paper money developed, but fiscal and military problems did not disappear."],"nuance":"Do not equate commercial growth directly with a modern European-style industrial revolution.","reflection":"Explain the connections between agricultural development and urban growth.","questions":[{"id":300004,"topic":"WHY03-0","type":"choice","era":"Tang–Song China","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY03","q":"Why can agricultural development support commerce?","answer":"Food and surplus production support larger populations and markets","options":["Food and surplus production support larger populations and markets","Agricultural growth always eliminates urban consumption","It removes every need to use money","It ends exchanges of different regional products"],"ex":"Food supplies and exchangeable surpluses help sustain cities, commerce and crafts.","refs":[{"label":"Columbia University · Song commercialization","url":"https://afe.easia.columbia.edu/songdynasty-module/econ-rev-commercial.html"}]},{"id":300005,"topic":"WHY03-1","type":"choice","era":"Tang–Song China","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY03","q":"How should Song economic development be understood?","answer":"Through interactions among production, transport, money and consumption","options":["Through interactions among production, transport, money and consumption","Paper money alone caused every change","Urban growth eliminated all state financial problems","Agriculture and maritime trade were unrelated changes"],"ex":"Several developments supported one another.","refs":[{"label":"Columbia University · Song commercialization","url":"https://afe.easia.columbia.edu/songdynasty-module/econ-rev-commercial.html"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"Columbia University · Song commercialization","url":"https://afe.easia.columbia.edu/songdynasty-module/econ-rev-commercial.html"}]},{"id":"WHY04","region":"east","period":"13th century","title":"Why could the Mongol Empire conquer so widely?","summary":"Steppe unification, military organization and the use of skills from conquered regions enabled sustained campaigns.","causes":[{"title":"Political unification","text":"Chinggis Khan reorganized different groups and strengthened unified command."},{"title":"Military organization","text":"Mounted tactics, mobility, intelligence gathering and discipline worked together."},{"title":"People and technology","text":"Craft workers, officials and siege techniques from conquered regions supported rule and warfare."}],"trigger":"Conflict with neighbouring powers and clashes over trade and diplomacy helped trigger expanding campaigns.","chain":["Unification of steppe forces","Organized campaigns and use of specialist skills","Wider rule and transport networks"],"consequences":["Conquest inflicted enormous destruction, while movement by merchants and envoys also expanded under Mongol rule.","Succession struggles and other tensions contributed to division into separate regimes."],"nuance":"Avoid explanations based on ethnic groups being naturally strong, or climate alone. Consider political choices, organization and regional differences.","reflection":"Name two factors that mounted tactics alone cannot explain.","questions":[{"id":300006,"topic":"WHY04-0","type":"choice","era":"13th century","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY04","q":"Which mechanism supported conquest?","answer":"Combining command structures, intelligence and technologies from conquered regions","options":["Combining command structures, intelligence and technologies from conquered regions","Always using only one type of weapon","Never attacking cities in farming regions","Banning the use of people from conquered territories"],"ex":"Mobility mattered alongside organization and the adoption of technologies.","refs":[{"label":"Columbia University · Mongol organization and conquest","url":"https://afe.easia.columbia.edu/mongols/"}]},{"id":300007,"topic":"WHY04-1","type":"choice","era":"13th century","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY04","q":"How should Mongol rule be assessed?","answer":"Consider both destructive conquest and expanding exchange under subsequent rule","options":["Consider both destructive conquest and expanding exchange under subsequent rule","Growing trade means conquest caused no harm","A vast empire could not experience succession struggles","Every region changed under an identical system"],"ex":"Expanded exchange does not cancel conquest violence or regional differences.","refs":[{"label":"Columbia University · Mongol organization and conquest","url":"https://afe.easia.columbia.edu/mongols/"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"Columbia University · Mongol organization and conquest","url":"https://afe.easia.columbia.edu/mongols/"}]},{"id":"WHY05","region":"east","period":"Ming China in the 15th century","title":"Why did Zheng He’s great voyages end?","summary":"The advantages the court saw in maritime expeditions changed alongside other spending and security priorities.","causes":[{"title":"Expedition objectives","text":"Court politics, including the Yongle Emperor’s prestige and the expansion of tributary relations, were major aims."},{"title":"Costs and burdens","text":"Building, maintaining and sending large fleets required considerable money and labour."},{"title":"Politics and defence","text":"Imperial and court support, and resource allocation for priorities such as northern defence, shaped continuation."}],"trigger":"After Zheng He’s final voyage, the state did not continue expeditions on the same scale.","chain":["Court-led great expeditions","Reassessment of financial, political and defence priorities","End of the state expeditions"],"consequences":["Ming foreign-policy priorities changed.","The end of state expeditions did not mean that all private maritime trade and coastal exchange disappeared."],"nuance":"It is a leap to conclude that China became completely isolated or suddenly lost its technology.","reflection":"Explain the policy change while distinguishing state expeditions from private trade.","questions":[{"id":300008,"topic":"WHY05-0","type":"choice","era":"Ming China in the 15th century","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY05","q":"What helps explain the end of the expeditions?","answer":"Changes in political support and resource allocation","options":["Changes in political support and resource allocation","Shipbuilding knowledge vanished overnight","Indian Ocean trade itself disappeared","Ming China had no coast"],"ex":"Relate court objectives and costs to changing defence priorities.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · From the Mamluks to Ming China","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-1/pages/17-2-from-the-mamluks-to-ming-china"}]},{"id":300009,"topic":"WHY05-1","type":"choice","era":"Ming China in the 15th century","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY05","q":"What cannot be concluded directly from the end of state expeditions?","answer":"Private maritime trade also vanished completely","options":["Private maritime trade also vanished completely","State resources devoted to great fleets declined","Court policies mattered to the expeditions","Foreign policy involved several possible choices"],"ex":"State activity is not the same as all social exchange.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · From the Mamluks to Ming China","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-1/pages/17-2-from-the-mamluks-to-ming-china"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · From the Mamluks to Ming China","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-1/pages/17-2-from-the-mamluks-to-ming-china"}]},{"id":"WHY06","region":"east","period":"First half of the 19th century","title":"Why did the Opium War break out?","summary":"Qing suppression of illegal opium trading clashed with British efforts to protect commercial interests through military force.","causes":[{"title":"Commercial interests","text":"The trade balance in goods such as tea and merchants’ interests formed part of the background."},{"title":"Opium’s effects","text":"Expanding smuggling brought addiction, silver outflows and other problems, prompting stronger Qing enforcement."},{"title":"States and force","text":"British trade demands and the decision to use military force turned the dispute into war."}],"trigger":"Tensions intensified after Lin Zexu’s confiscation and destruction of opium, and Britain pursued military action.","chain":["Opium trade and conflicting interests","Qing enforcement and British military action","War and unequal treaties"],"consequences":["The Treaty of Nanjing and related arrangements imposed the opening of ports and the cession of Hong Kong Island on the Qing.","Unequal power over trade and sovereignty became a major issue in later reforms and foreign relations."],"nuance":"Explaining trade disputes does not justify the opium trade or aggression.","reflection":"Distinguish the economic background from the political decision to go to war.","questions":[{"id":300010,"topic":"WHY06-0","type":"choice","era":"First half of the 19th century","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY06","q":"Which sequence appropriately explains the war?","answer":"British force escalated conflict over opium trading and Qing enforcement","options":["British force escalated conflict over opium trading and Qing enforcement","Treaty of Nanjing ports opened before the opium confiscation","Qing occupation of Britain was the direct cause","The trade balance automatically caused war"],"ex":"Separate underlying interests from decisions to use force.","refs":[{"label":"National Army Museum · First Opium War","url":"https://www.nam.ac.uk/explore/first-china-war-1839-1842"}]},{"id":300011,"topic":"WHY06-1","type":"choice","era":"First half of the 19th century","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY06","q":"What matters when explaining economic motives?","answer":"Explaining commercial interests is different from justifying actions","options":["Explaining commercial interests is different from justifying actions","Profit always makes war legitimate","Signing a treaty always makes sovereignty equal","Opium’s harms have nothing to do with economic history"],"ex":"Understand interests while also recognizing unequal power and harm.","refs":[{"label":"National Army Museum · First Opium War","url":"https://www.nam.ac.uk/explore/first-china-war-1839-1842"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"National Army Museum · First Opium War","url":"https://www.nam.ac.uk/explore/first-china-war-1839-1842"}]},{"id":"WHY07","region":"east","period":"19th-century Japan","title":"Why did the Meiji Restoration lead to institutional reform?","summary":"Foreign pressure and tensions within the shogunate–domain system prompted the new government to reorganize rule, finance and the military.","causes":[{"title":"Foreign pressure","text":"The opening of ports and unequal treaties heightened diplomatic and security concerns."},{"title":"Domestic change","text":"Financial difficulties and changes in distribution and society challenged the shogunate–domain system."},{"title":"Political coalitions","text":"Anti-shogunate forces and political activity around the imperial court helped form a new regime."}],"trigger":"After the shogunate lost power and the new government formed, reforms included replacing domains with prefectures and changing taxation and the military.","chain":["Domestic and external crises and political conflict","Formation of a new government","Centralization and modern institutions"],"consequences":["State systems for collecting revenue and mobilizing troops nationwide became stronger.","Reforms did not benefit everyone equally: heavier burdens and the loss of established rights also provoked resistance."],"nuance":"Saying that Japan simply copied the West overlooks the combination of domestic institutions and political choices.","reflection":"How did centralization change both the power to reform and the burdens on people?","questions":[{"id":300012,"topic":"WHY07-0","type":"choice","era":"19th-century Japan","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY07","q":"What was a purpose of early Meiji institutional reform?","answer":"Reorganizing finance, the military and government nationwide","options":["Reorganizing finance, the military and government nationwide","Permanently granting domains independent diplomatic powers","Adopting every foreign institution at once without selection","Ending all connections between taxation and the state"],"ex":"The government sought nationwide mobilization and reorganization of rule.","refs":[{"label":"Stanford SPICE · Domestic and external contexts of the Meiji Restoration","url":"https://web.stanford.edu/group/spice/road_to_tokyo/lessons/Meiji.pdf"}]},{"id":300013,"topic":"WHY07-1","type":"choice","era":"19th-century Japan","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY07","q":"Which view of the reforms’ effects is appropriate?","answer":"Consider stronger state capacity and different burdens on different groups","options":["Consider stronger state capacity and different burdens on different groups","Centralization imposed no new burdens on anyone","Foreign pressure alone explains domestic politics","Introducing institutions and establishing them socially happened at the same instant"],"ex":"Distinguish beneficiaries and burden-bearers, and introduction from longer-term establishment.","refs":[{"label":"Stanford SPICE · Domestic and external contexts of the Meiji Restoration","url":"https://web.stanford.edu/group/spice/road_to_tokyo/lessons/Meiji.pdf"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"Stanford SPICE · Domestic and external contexts of the Meiji Restoration","url":"https://web.stanford.edu/group/spice/road_to_tokyo/lessons/Meiji.pdf"}]},{"id":"WHY08","region":"east","period":"Korea in the late 19th century","title":"Why did Korea’s opening become an international conflict?","summary":"Opening Korea changed not only bilateral commerce, but relations among the Qing, Japan, other powers and Korean political groups.","causes":[{"title":"International relations","text":"Japan and Western expansion overlapped with Korea’s traditional relationship with the Qing."},{"title":"Trade and sovereignty","text":"Unequal conditions in treaties such as the Japan–Korea Treaty of Amity created disputes over commerce and state powers."},{"title":"Domestic choices","text":"Disagreements over reform and foreign policy became linked to foreign intervention."}],"trigger":"A treaty was concluded under Japanese military pressure, and competing foreign influences continued afterwards.","chain":["Opening and unequal treaty relationships","Domestic reform and foreign intervention","Growing international conflict over Korea"],"consequences":["Movements for Korean political reform and independent sovereignty developed.","Competition involving Japan and the Qing helped reshape East Asia’s international order."],"nuance":"Do not treat Koreans as merely moved around by great powers: examine their diverse plans, choices and resistance.","reflection":"Explain how foreign relations and domestic politics could influence each other.","questions":[{"id":300014,"topic":"WHY08-0","type":"choice","era":"Korea in the late 19th century","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY08","q":"What helps explain Korea’s opening?","answer":"Interactions among treaty terms, neighbouring powers’ competition and domestic politics","options":["Interactions among treaty terms, neighbouring powers’ competition and domestic politics","Foreign political influence always disappears once trade starts","There were no different reform proposals within Korea","Relations with the Qing alone explain every later development"],"ex":"Opening changed domestic and external relationships together.","refs":[{"label":"Columbia University · Korea’s opening and modern reforms","url":"https://afe.easia.columbia.edu/ps/korea/independent_water.pdf"}]},{"id":300015,"topic":"WHY08-1","type":"choice","era":"Korea in the late 19th century","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY08","q":"How should historical agency be considered?","answer":"Pay attention to Korean reforms, resistance and foreign-policy choices","options":["Pay attention to Korean reforms, resistance and foreign-policy choices","All Korean groups supported the same foreign policy","Foreign intervention makes domestic politics meaningless","The treaty word independent alone proves actual equality"],"ex":"Distinguish treaty language, real power relations and domestic action.","refs":[{"label":"Columbia University · Korea’s opening and modern reforms","url":"https://afe.easia.columbia.edu/ps/korea/independent_water.pdf"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"Columbia University · Korea’s opening and modern reforms","url":"https://afe.easia.columbia.edu/ps/korea/independent_water.pdf"}]},{"id":"WHY09","region":"sea","period":"Southeast Asia in the 14th–15th centuries","title":"Why did Angkor lose its central position?","summary":"Changes in water management, population, politics and trade are best understood as a long, interacting process.","causes":[{"title":"City and water management","text":"Maintaining large water systems was essential to supporting the extensive city and its agriculture."},{"title":"Environmental variability","text":"Droughts and intense rainfall may have placed stress on water infrastructure."},{"title":"Politics and exchange","text":"Shifts in political centres, population movement and a turn towards maritime trade also played roles."}],"trigger":"Urban activity and population appear to have been changing before military conflict; warfare was not the only turning point.","chain":["Multiple environmental and social pressures","Changes in urban functions and population","Movement of the political centre"],"consequences":["Royal centres moved southwards.","Declining centrality did not mean that all local life and religious activity vanished at once."],"nuance":"Overnight collapse from a single invasion or drought alone fails to capture the complexity of the research.","reflection":"What institutions and human actions mediate the effects of environmental change?","questions":[{"id":300016,"topic":"WHY09-0","type":"choice","era":"Southeast Asia in the 14th–15th centuries","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY09","q":"What appropriately explains Angkor’s transformation?","answer":"Combine long-term environmental, water-management, political and population changes","options":["Combine long-term environmental, water-management, political and population changes","Assume every inhabitant vanished after one battle","Climate change makes political choices irrelevant","A moving centre always means an entire civilization disappeared"],"ex":"Several factors over a long period affected the decline in centrality.","refs":[{"label":"Penny et al. · Angkor’s gradual decline (PNAS, 2019)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1821460116"}]},{"id":300017,"topic":"WHY09-1","type":"choice","era":"Southeast Asia in the 14th–15th centuries","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY09","q":"What should supplement an environmental explanation?","answer":"Water management and political responses shaped the effects","options":["Water management and political responses shaped the effects","The environment always produces the same result in every society","Water systems and population movements were unrelated","Surviving temples prove urban functions never changed"],"ex":"Relate natural conditions to institutions and human responses.","refs":[{"label":"Penny et al. · Angkor’s gradual decline (PNAS, 2019)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1821460116"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"Penny et al. · Angkor’s gradual decline (PNAS, 2019)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1821460116"}]},{"id":"WHY10","region":"sea","period":"Maritime Asia in the 15th–16th centuries","title":"Why did Melaka become a trading hub?","summary":"The strait’s location combined with institutions that enabled merchants to gather and trade.","causes":[{"title":"Geography and monsoons","text":"Its strait linked the Indian Ocean and South China Sea and provided a stopping point while sailors waited for monsoon winds."},{"title":"Merchant networks","text":"Traders from different regions gathered to exchange goods and information."},{"title":"Port institutions","text":"Rulers’ management of trade and relations with merchants supported the port’s functions."}],"trigger":"The port city grew within extensive maritime networks; its strategic importance also attracted Portuguese expansion.","chain":["Strait location and monsoon navigation","Concentration of merchants, goods and information","Port-city prosperity and competition among powers"],"consequences":["Religions and cultures, including Islam, spread alongside commerce.","States competed more intensely to control the strategic strait."],"nuance":"Location did not automatically guarantee prosperity: institutions and trust among merchants also mattered.","reflection":"Explain how geography and human institutions interacted.","questions":[{"id":300018,"topic":"WHY10-0","type":"choice","era":"Maritime Asia in the 15th–16th centuries","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY10","q":"Which combination explains Melaka’s development?","answer":"Strait location, monsoons, merchants and port institutions","options":["Strait location, monsoons, merchants and port institutions","Location alone, with politics and merchants irrelevant","Ships never stopping and every voyage being direct","Relying only on land agriculture and avoiding foreign trade"],"ex":"Connect natural conditions with human activity.","refs":[{"label":"UNESCO · Melaka and strait trade","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1223/"}]},{"id":300019,"topic":"WHY10-1","type":"choice","era":"Maritime Asia in the 15th–16th centuries","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY10","q":"How did port-city development relate to culture?","answer":"Merchants’ movement also supported exchanges of religion and knowledge","options":["Merchants’ movement also supported exchanges of religion and knowledge","Trade and cultural movement were always separate","Port growth always removed foreign powers’ interest","Monsoons did not affect navigation"],"ex":"Trade involved movement of people and information as well as goods.","refs":[{"label":"UNESCO · Melaka and strait trade","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1223/"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"UNESCO · Melaka and strait trade","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1223/"}]},{"id":"WHY11","region":"south","period":"Ancient–medieval Asia","title":"Why did Buddhism spread beyond India?","summary":"Travelling monks, translation, trade networks and local patrons combined to produce regional forms of Buddhism.","causes":[{"title":"Movement and transport","text":"Monks and merchants travelled through inland oases and along sea routes."},{"title":"Translation and learning","text":"Scriptural translation and scholarly exchange carried teachings into different language communities."},{"title":"Social support","text":"Rulers, merchants and local believers provided protection and support for temples and religious activities."}],"trigger":"Repeated missionary journeys and travels in search of teachings helped practices and ideas take root in local societies.","chain":["Travel and translation","Local support and learning","Regional establishment and transformation"],"consequences":["Exchanges of Buddhist art, architecture and thought expanded.","Reception varied by region and interacted with existing beliefs and politics."],"nuance":"Do not imagine Indian teachings being copied unchanged: examine translation, selection and transformation.","reflection":"Why does the movement of objects alone not explain how a religion becomes established?","questions":[{"id":300020,"topic":"WHY11-0","type":"choice","era":"Ancient–medieval Asia","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY11","q":"What relationships supported Buddhism’s spread?","answer":"Transport networks, translation and local support worked together","options":["Transport networks, translation and local support worked together","Once scriptures arrived, translation and support were unnecessary","Every region adopted identical institutions and rituals","Trade and monks’ journeys never intersected"],"ex":"Consider both conditions for movement and the societies receiving teachings.","refs":[{"label":"UNESCO · Silk Road exchanges","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/silk-roads/about-silk-roads"},{"label":"OpenStax · Vedic India and the Maurya Empire","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-1/pages/5-4-vedic-india-to-the-fall-of-the-maurya-empire"}]},{"id":300021,"topic":"WHY11-1","type":"choice","era":"Ancient–medieval Asia","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY11","q":"How should reception be understood?","answer":"Buddhism changed within local languages, beliefs and politics","options":["Buddhism changed within local languages, beliefs and politics","Any change from an original means no transmission occurred","Where rulers supported Buddhism there were no ordinary believers","Existing cultures disappeared immediately when teachings arrived"],"ex":"Transmission also involves translation and reinterpretation.","refs":[{"label":"UNESCO · Silk Road exchanges","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/silk-roads/about-silk-roads"},{"label":"OpenStax · Vedic India and the Maurya Empire","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-1/pages/5-4-vedic-india-to-the-fall-of-the-maurya-empire"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"UNESCO · Silk Road exchanges","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/silk-roads/about-silk-roads"},{"label":"OpenStax · Vedic India and the Maurya Empire","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-1/pages/5-4-vedic-india-to-the-fall-of-the-maurya-empire"}]},{"id":"WHY12","region":"south","period":"20th-century South Asia","title":"Why did Indian independence also involve partition?","summary":"A long independence struggle and changing colonial rule coincided with deepening disputes over power in the future states.","causes":[{"title":"Independence movements","text":"Organizations including the Indian National Congress and mass movements put pressure on British rule."},{"title":"World war","text":"War increased Britain’s financial and military burdens and affected its postwar policies."},{"title":"Representation and power","text":"Congress, the Muslim League and others disputed the future state and the position of minorities."}],"trigger":"Postwar negotiations and political conflict led to the separate independence of India and Pakistan in 1947.","chain":["Independence demands and weakening colonial control","Conflicting visions of future states","Partition and large-scale migration"],"consequences":["Colonial rule ended, while population movements and violence caused immense suffering.","Disputes over borders and interstate relations remained."],"nuance":"Avoid assuming that religious difference had always made partition inevitable; examine modern institutions and political processes.","reflection":"How can independence and the harms of partition be explained within the same history?","questions":[{"id":300022,"topic":"WHY12-0","type":"choice","era":"20th-century South Asia","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY12","q":"Which combination helps explain independence?","answer":"Independence movements, wartime burdens and postwar negotiations","options":["Independence movements, wartime burdens and postwar negotiations","Britain’s postwar burdens alone, with mass movements irrelevant","Gandhi alone completed the entire process","Religious difference produces identical borders in every period"],"ex":"Relate the people organizing movements to international and institutional conditions.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · Global Tensions and Decolonization","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/14-4-global-tensions-and-decolonization"}]},{"id":300023,"topic":"WHY12-1","type":"choice","era":"20th-century South Asia","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY12","q":"Which understanding of partition is appropriate?","answer":"Competing state visions and disputes over representation mattered","options":["Competing state visions and disputes over representation mattered","Different religions must always form separate states","Independence prevented migration and violence","Colonial political institutions did not affect independence negotiations"],"ex":"Examine the formation of political conflict, not religion’s existence alone.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · Global Tensions and Decolonization","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/14-4-global-tensions-and-decolonization"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · Global Tensions and Decolonization","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/14-4-global-tensions-and-decolonization"}]},{"id":"WHY13","region":"islam","period":"West Asia in the 7th–8th centuries","title":"Why did the early Islamic states expand?","summary":"Community integration and military-political organization combined with conditions in neighbouring empires.","causes":[{"title":"Community integration","text":"The religious community provided a framework linking groups within Arabia."},{"title":"Military leadership","text":"Under caliphs and other leaders, campaigns and systems of rule were organized."},{"title":"Neighbouring conditions","text":"Long Byzantine–Sasanian wars and circumstances within local societies helped create opportunities for expansion."}],"trigger":"Military campaigns extended beyond the Arabian Peninsula and expanded rule across West Asia and elsewhere.","chain":["Formation of community and military organization","Conquest of neighbouring territories","Government over wide territories and gradual religious change"],"consequences":["Wider connections developed in commerce, administration and scholarship.","Conversion among conquered populations was neither uniform nor immediate; regions experienced different, lengthy processes."],"nuance":"State military expansion and individuals’ adoption of Islam were not the same event.","reflection":"Why might rapid conquest and the establishment of a religion proceed at different speeds?","questions":[{"id":300024,"topic":"WHY13-0","type":"choice","era":"West Asia in the 7th–8th centuries","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY13","q":"Which combination explains early expansion?","answer":"Community integration, military organization and neighbouring imperial conditions","options":["Community integration, military organization and neighbouring imperial conditions","Unanimous conversion alone expanded territory","Nearby wars and politics had no connection with expansion","Every region had identical administration before the campaigns"],"ex":"Consider both internal and external conditions.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · Islamization and Religious Rule","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-1/pages/11-3-islamization-and-religious-rule-under-islam"}]},{"id":300025,"topic":"WHY13-1","type":"choice","era":"West Asia in the 7th–8th centuries","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY13","q":"How did conquest relate to conversion?","answer":"Political control and religious reception could change at different speeds","options":["Political control and religious reception could change at different speeds","Every inhabitant always converted on the day of conquest","Islamic states contained no non-Muslims","Without simultaneous conversion there was no state expansion"],"ex":"Political boundaries and people’s beliefs need not change together.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · Islamization and Religious Rule","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-1/pages/11-3-islamization-and-religious-rule-under-islam"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · Islamization and Religious Rule","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-1/pages/11-3-islamization-and-religious-rule-under-islam"}]},{"id":"WHY14","region":"islam","period":"Abbasid period","title":"Why did scholarship flourish in Baghdad?","summary":"Commercial wealth, rulers’ and other patrons’ support, and an urban meeting of languages and intellectual traditions reinforced one another.","causes":[{"title":"City and trade","text":"People, goods and books arrived from many regions, while activities requiring specialist knowledge expanded."},{"title":"Patronage and demand","text":"Courts and powerful patrons supported scholars and translators and sought medical, astronomical and administrative knowledge."},{"title":"Diverse participants","text":"People of different religions and languages translated and developed Greek, Iranian, Indian and other learning."}],"trigger":"Sustained translation and research supported criticism and improvement of existing knowledge as well as new investigations.","chain":["Concentration of people, wealth and books","Support for translation and research","Reorganization and wider transmission of knowledge"],"consequences":["Research developed in mathematics, medicine, astronomy and other fields.","Several cultural regions contributed to knowledge that later travelled elsewhere."],"nuance":"Do not reduce these achievements to the genius of one religion or ethnicity, or to merely preserving ancient works.","reflection":"Explain the roles of cities, institutions and translation in developing knowledge.","questions":[{"id":300026,"topic":"WHY14-0","type":"choice","era":"Abbasid period","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY14","q":"What supported scholarly development?","answer":"Commercial-city exchange, patrons, multilingual translation and research","options":["Commercial-city exchange, patrons, multilingual translation and research","Completely cutting off knowledge from other regions","Making one ruler responsible for all learning","Only copying classical works without criticism"],"ex":"Knowledge developed through exchange among participants and social conditions supporting research.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · Islamization and Religious Rule","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-1/pages/11-3-islamization-and-religious-rule-under-islam"}]},{"id":300027,"topic":"WHY14-1","type":"choice","era":"Abbasid period","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY14","q":"What was a role of translation?","answer":"Linking language communities and encouraging new research","options":["Linking language communities and encouraging new research","Preventing all later criticism or improvement","Ensuring all translators shared one religion and language","Remaining unrelated to trade and political support"],"ex":"Translation was intellectual exchange, not simply passive copying.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · Islamization and Religious Rule","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-1/pages/11-3-islamization-and-religious-rule-under-islam"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · Islamization and Religious Rule","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-1/pages/11-3-islamization-and-religious-rule-under-islam"}]},{"id":"WHY15","region":"europe","period":"Europe in the 14th–16th centuries","title":"Why did Renaissance art flourish in cities?","summary":"Urban wealth and competition, interest in classical culture and patrons’ objectives supported artists’ work.","causes":[{"title":"Commerce and wealth","text":"Wealth accumulated through trade and finance provided funds for artistic production."},{"title":"Patrons’ aims","text":"Churches, rulers, cities and merchants commissioned works to express faith and prestige."},{"title":"Learning and exchange","text":"Classical studies, workshop skills and interregional contacts supported changing artistic expression."}],"trigger":"Artists produced and experimented amid commissions and competition among cities and powerful patrons.","chain":["Urban wealth and commissions","Workshops, learning and artistic experimentation","Developing artistic culture and interregional exchange"],"consequences":["New forms of expression appeared in architecture, sculpture, painting and other media.","Religious works remained numerous alongside secular subjects."],"nuance":"Do not define the Renaissance as a period when religion vanished and individuals suddenly became free.","reflection":"What can studying a patron reveal beyond studying the artist alone?","questions":[{"id":300028,"topic":"WHY15-0","type":"choice","era":"Europe in the 14th–16th centuries","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY15","q":"What combination helps explain an artwork’s production?","answer":"The artist’s skill, patrons’ aims and funds, and the social setting","options":["The artist’s skill, patrons’ aims and funds, and the social setting","Talent alone, with funding and commissions irrelevant","Only the disappearance of religious works","Prohibiting exchanges among cities"],"ex":"Examine the concrete relationships in which works were made.","refs":[{"label":"Smarthistory · Renaissance patrons","url":"https://smarthistory.org/types-of-renaissance-patronage/"}]},{"id":300029,"topic":"WHY15-1","type":"choice","era":"Europe in the 14th–16th centuries","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY15","q":"Why study patrons?","answer":"To understand how works expressed purposes such as faith or prestige","options":["To understand how works expressed purposes such as faith or prestige","To prove artists had no room for invention","To prove all works shared one political meaning","To explain a work completely through its price"],"ex":"Consider why a work was wanted and who would see it, as well as its funding.","refs":[{"label":"Smarthistory · Renaissance patrons","url":"https://smarthistory.org/types-of-renaissance-patronage/"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"Smarthistory · Renaissance patrons","url":"https://smarthistory.org/types-of-renaissance-patronage/"}]},{"id":"WHY16","region":"europe","period":"16th-century Europe","title":"Why did the Reformation become a political issue?","summary":"Religious criticism spread through print and became linked to rulers’ and cities’ interests.","causes":[{"title":"Faith and criticism","text":"Questions about salvation and the Church attracted attention."},{"title":"Print and debate","text":"Pamphlets and translations spread ideas and drew more participants into controversy."},{"title":"Power and institutions","text":"Princes’ and cities’ choices involved territorial government, Church property and authority."}],"trigger":"Responses to Luther and others widened conflicts involving churches and political authorities.","chain":["Religious challenges","Printed and preached debate and political support","Reorganization of churches and states and religious conflict"],"consequences":["Multiple denominations formed and Catholic reforms also developed.","Education, worship and relations between states and religion changed, amid prolonged warfare and repression."],"nuance":"Do not reduce belief to economic interests alone. Distinguish conditions for spreading ideas from political involvement.","reflection":"Why did printing not necessarily lead everyone to the same religious views?","questions":[{"id":300030,"topic":"WHY16-0","type":"choice","era":"16th-century Europe","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY16","q":"Why did reform become political?","answer":"Debates about faith became linked to power and institutional interests","options":["Debates about faith became linked to power and institutional interests","Church questions were wholly unrelated to political power","Printing automatically proved only one argument correct","All princes and cities responded identically"],"ex":"Examine relations between ideas and the politics supporting or suppressing them.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · The Protestant Reformation","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/5-1-the-protestant-reformation"}]},{"id":300031,"topic":"WHY16-1","type":"choice","era":"16th-century Europe","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY16","q":"How should printing’s role be understood?","answer":"It spread differing claims and broadened controversy","options":["It spread differing claims and broadened controversy","Printing technology alone removed religious conflict","Wider information makes everyone choose the same interpretation","All oral preaching immediately disappeared"],"ex":"Wider circulation and agreement are different things.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · The Protestant Reformation","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/5-1-the-protestant-reformation"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · The Protestant Reformation","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/5-1-the-protestant-reformation"}]},{"id":"WHY17","region":"europe","period":"Britain in the 18th–19th centuries","title":"Why did Britain industrialize first?","summary":"Resources, markets, finance, labour and technical improvements combined to support expanding mechanization.","causes":[{"title":"Resources and technology","text":"Coal use and improvements in spinning and power changed production methods."},{"title":"Markets and finance","text":"Domestic and overseas markets, trade, finance and investment supported expanding output."},{"title":"Agriculture and labour","text":"Changes in agriculture, population and everyday life interacted with food supplies and factory labour."}],"trigger":"Mechanization advanced in cotton manufacturing and spread into other sectors alongside improvements in power and transport.","chain":["Markets, investment and technical improvement","Spread of factories and powered production","Urbanization and changes in work and everyday life"],"consequences":["Output and transport capacity grew alongside harsh labour conditions and urban environmental problems.","Overseas raw materials, colonialism and slavery were also part of the world-economic connections."],"nuance":"Coal or a single invention alone does not make an industrial revolution inevitable.","reflection":"Why does even a useful invention need favourable conditions to become widespread?","questions":[{"id":300032,"topic":"WHY17-0","type":"choice","era":"Britain in the 18th–19th centuries","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY17","q":"Which combination explains Britain’s early industrialization?","answer":"Resources, markets, finance, labour and technology","options":["Resources, markets, finance, labour and technology","The steam engine instantly changed every industry at once","Domestic agriculture and overseas markets were irrelevant","Factories eliminated social problems"],"ex":"Distinguish an invention from conditions linking it to investment and production.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · The First Industrial Revolution","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/6-3-capitalism-and-the-first-industrial-revolution"}]},{"id":300033,"topic":"WHY17-1","type":"choice","era":"Britain in the 18th–19th centuries","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY17","q":"How should industrialization’s results be understood?","answer":"Consider growing production and labour and environmental burdens","options":["Consider growing production and labour and environmental burdens","More output instantly improves every social group’s life equally","Urban change was unrelated to factory growth","Overseas raw-material supplies had no world-economic significance"],"ex":"Benefits and burdens varied across groups and over time.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · The First Industrial Revolution","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/6-3-capitalism-and-the-first-industrial-revolution"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · The First Industrial Revolution","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/6-3-capitalism-and-the-first-industrial-revolution"}]},{"id":"WHY18","region":"europe","period":"France in the late 18th century","title":"Why did the French Revolution occur?","summary":"A state financial impasse linked conflicts over estate privilege and representation to a political crisis.","causes":[{"title":"Financial crisis","text":"War and debt burdens met difficulties in reforming taxation."},{"title":"Society and daily life","text":"Resentment of estate privilege combined with high food prices and insecurity."},{"title":"Political demands","text":"Arguments about representation and rights intensified conflict with royal power and privileged estates."}],"trigger":"After the Estates-General met, disputes over voting and popular action unsettled the existing order.","chain":["Financial difficulties and social grievances","Political conflict over representation","Revolution and reorganization of institutions and rights"],"consequences":["Changes included abolishing feudal privileges and proclaiming the Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen.","Revolution was not a single forward path: it involved war, conflict, violence and repeated changes in power."],"nuance":"Neither Enlightenment ideas alone nor hunger alone explains it. Ask what turned a crisis into political transformation.","reflection":"Explain how a financial problem became a problem of national representation.","questions":[{"id":300034,"topic":"WHY18-0","type":"choice","era":"France in the late 18th century","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY18","q":"How did financial crisis connect with revolution?","answer":"Tax reform became entangled with estate privilege and representation","options":["Tax reform became entangled with estate privilege and representation","Every financial crisis necessarily produces the same revolution","The spread of ideas meant there were no material grievances","Summoning the Estates-General resolved every conflict"],"ex":"Connect finance to social institutions and political participation.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · Revolutions: America, France, and Haiti","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/7-3-revolutions-america-france-and-haiti"}]},{"id":300035,"topic":"WHY18-1","type":"choice","era":"France in the late 18th century","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY18","q":"How should the revolution’s course be understood?","answer":"Groups pursued different aims and outcomes changed","options":["Groups pursued different aims and outcomes changed","Every participant anticipated the same ending from the start","The Declaration of Rights immediately removed every exclusion","The revolution was entirely domestic and unrelated to war"],"ex":"Do not treat revolution as the execution of one predetermined plan.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · Revolutions: America, France, and Haiti","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/7-3-revolutions-america-france-and-haiti"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · Revolutions: America, France, and Haiti","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/7-3-revolutions-america-france-and-haiti"}]},{"id":"WHY19","region":"africa","period":"Medieval West Africa","title":"Why did the Mali Empire become wealthy?","summary":"Mali connected gold-producing and other regions to trans-Saharan routes and drew these flows into rule and public revenue.","causes":[{"title":"Trade connections","text":"Regional goods and people gathered on routes linking north and south of the Sahara."},{"title":"Resources and demand","text":"West African gold and demand for goods such as salt supported long-distance exchange."},{"title":"Systems of rule","text":"Managing and taxing trade, together with relations with local societies, supported royal power."}],"trigger":"Expanding territorial and commercial influence, and events such as Mansa Musa’s pilgrimage, strengthened external connections.","chain":["Resources and trans-Saharan trade","Trade management, taxation and royal power","Growth of cities, scholarship and diplomacy"],"consequences":["Trading towns and Islamic scholarly activities developed.","Concentrated wealth remained vulnerable to changing routes and political conflicts; it was not permanently secure."],"nuance":"Do not treat Africa as merely receiving outside influence. Consider merchants, rulers and local societies as active participants.","reflection":"How does possessing resources differ from a state becoming wealthy?","questions":[{"id":300036,"topic":"WHY19-0","type":"choice","era":"Medieval West Africa","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY19","q":"What relationship supported Mali’s wealth?","answer":"Resources, trade routes, government and taxation","options":["Resources, trade routes, government and taxation","Gold alone made politics and trade routes unnecessary","The Sahara blocked every movement between regions","Only foreign merchants drove politics and the economy"],"ex":"Institutions linked resources to trade and state revenue.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · Medieval Sub-Saharan Africa","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-1/pages/15-2-medieval-sub-saharan-africa"}]},{"id":300037,"topic":"WHY19-1","type":"choice","era":"Medieval West Africa","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY19","q":"Which view of Mali’s history is appropriate?","answer":"Recognize local actors’ role in creating long-distance exchange","options":["Recognize local actors’ role in creating long-distance exchange","Islam’s reception removed all roles for existing societies","Trade wealth was distributed equally to everyone","Wealth made changing trade routes irrelevant"],"ex":"Examine the people making exchanges and differences within society.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · Medieval Sub-Saharan Africa","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-1/pages/15-2-medieval-sub-saharan-africa"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · Medieval Sub-Saharan Africa","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-1/pages/15-2-medieval-sub-saharan-africa"}]},{"id":"WHY20","region":"africa","period":"After the Second World War","title":"Why did African independence advance?","summary":"Local organization and movements combined with the postwar international order and constraints on colonial rulers.","causes":[{"title":"Independence movements","text":"Workers, farmers, intellectuals, parties and other groups demanded rights and independence."},{"title":"Constraints on colonial powers","text":"War damage, financial costs and resistance made colonial rule harder to maintain."},{"title":"International conditions","text":"The UN, anticolonialism and Cold War rivalries affected independence negotiations."}],"trigger":"Different regions pursued independence through negotiations, elections, mass movements, armed struggle and other routes.","chain":["Local demands and crises of colonial government","Negotiation, movements, conflict and international pressure","Formation of independent states"],"consequences":["More sovereign states changed the composition of international society.","Economic structures, borders and distributions of domestic power remained challenges after independence."],"nuance":"European weakness alone does not explain local movements, and not every territory followed the same route.","reflection":"Distinguish achieving independence from the challenges that followed.","questions":[{"id":300038,"topic":"WHY20-0","type":"choice","era":"After the Second World War","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY20","q":"What combination explains the advance of independence?","answer":"Local movements, constraints on colonial powers and the international setting","options":["Local movements, constraints on colonial powers and the international setting","Colonial goodwill alone granted everyone independence at once","The Cold War made local action meaningless","Every region became independent in the same year by the same means"],"ex":"Combine the actions of colonized peoples with international conditions.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · Global Tensions and Decolonization","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/14-4-global-tensions-and-decolonization"}]},{"id":300039,"topic":"WHY20-1","type":"choice","era":"After the Second World War","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY20","q":"Which statement about the post-independence period is appropriate?","answer":"Economic and state-building challenges remained after legal independence","options":["Economic and state-building challenges remained after legal independence","Changing the flag instantly erased colonial economic structures","All independent states necessarily adopted one political system","International rivalries completely ceased to matter"],"ex":"Sovereignty and social-economic reorganization need not advance at the same speed.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · Global Tensions and Decolonization","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/14-4-global-tensions-and-decolonization"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · Global Tensions and Decolonization","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/14-4-global-tensions-and-decolonization"}]},{"id":"WHY21","region":"americas","period":"The Americas in the 16th century","title":"Why could small Spanish forces advance their conquests?","summary":"Disease, conflicts and alliances within Indigenous societies, military action and supplies interacted.","causes":[{"title":"Local politics","text":"Some groups opposed to powers such as the Aztec Empire allied with the Spanish."},{"title":"Disease and population","text":"Introduced diseases killed many people and deeply disrupted politics and production."},{"title":"Military action","text":"Weapons, horses and tactics mattered alongside allied troops, supplies and the capture of leaders."}],"trigger":"Warfare and political crises following contact unfolded under differing regional conditions.","chain":["Contact, alliances and disease","Military action and political disruption","Conquest and the formation of colonial rule"],"consequences":["Violence, forced labour and disease caused great harm while societies and religions were reorganized.","Indigenous resistance and negotiation continued: conquest did not end their agency."],"nuance":"Explanations based on guns alone, or treating Indigenous peoples as a single group, miss alliances and regional differences.","reflection":"Name two conditions that military technology alone cannot explain.","questions":[{"id":300040,"topic":"WHY21-0","type":"choice","era":"The Americas in the 16th century","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY21","q":"How should conquest be explained?","answer":"Relate disease, local alliances, military action and supplies","options":["Relate disease, local alliances, military action and supplies","Explain the entire process through a few Europeans’ weapons alone","All Indigenous powers had identical political aims","Disease was unrelated to political and economic change"],"ex":"Recognize multiple actors and different regional conditions.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · Crossing the Atlantic","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/5-2-crossing-the-atlantic"}]},{"id":300041,"topic":"WHY21-1","type":"choice","era":"The Americas in the 16th century","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY21","q":"Which view of Indigenous agency is appropriate?","answer":"People engaged in diverse forms of resistance, alliance and negotiation","options":["People engaged in diverse forms of resistance, alliance and negotiation","All Indigenous actions and cultures disappeared after conquest","Groups allied with Spain had no independent aims","Indigenous societies had no differences of interest"],"ex":"Understand alliances and resistance through each group’s aims and circumstances.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · Crossing the Atlantic","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/5-2-crossing-the-atlantic"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · Crossing the Atlantic","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/5-2-crossing-the-atlantic"}]},{"id":"WHY22","region":"americas","period":"Late 18th–early 19th centuries","title":"Why did the Haitian Revolution lead to abolition and independence?","summary":"Enslaved people’s actions connected colonial contradictions, revolutionary rights debates and warfare.","causes":[{"title":"Colonial structures","text":"Wealth-producing plantations depended on brutal enslaved labour and racial inequality."},{"title":"Conflicts over rights","text":"The French Revolution sharpened questions about who should receive rights."},{"title":"Resistance and international war","text":"Uprisings and organization by enslaved people became entangled with European wars and intervention."}],"trigger":"After the great uprising of 1791, struggles over abolition and restoration of slavery and resistance to French troops led towards independence.","chain":["Resistance to slavery and demands for rights","Clashes among colonial, metropolitan and foreign forces","An independent state rejecting slavery"],"consequences":["Haitian independence posed a major challenge to slavery and racial hierarchy in the Atlantic world.","The independent state also faced diplomatic isolation, indemnity payments and other heavy burdens."],"nuance":"Look beyond freedom supposedly bestowed by French revolutionary ideals to people’s own actions in pursuing it.","reflection":"How could the same ideal of liberty inspire different demands from different positions?","questions":[{"id":300042,"topic":"WHY22-0","type":"choice","era":"Late 18th–early 19th centuries","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY22","q":"Which explanation of the Haitian Revolution is appropriate?","answer":"Connect participants’ uprisings, colonial contradictions and warfare","options":["Connect participants’ uprisings, colonial contradictions and warfare","The metropole simply volunteered independence","French revolutionary rights applied unconditionally and identically to everyone","Colonial wealth had no connection to enslaved labour"],"ex":"Place people seeking freedom at the centre while relating complex political circumstances.","refs":[{"label":"US Office of the Historian · Haitian Revolution","url":"https://history.state.gov/milestones/1784-1800/haitian-rev"}]},{"id":300043,"topic":"WHY22-1","type":"choice","era":"Late 18th–early 19th centuries","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY22","q":"How should independence’s results be assessed?","answer":"Consider both the rejection of slavery and post-independence constraints","options":["Consider both the rejection of slavery and post-independence constraints","Independence solved all diplomatic and economic problems","External pressures vanished after independence","The revolution mattered only for politics in France"],"ex":"Recognize a momentous change alongside subsequent difficulties.","refs":[{"label":"US Office of the Historian · Haitian Revolution","url":"https://history.state.gov/milestones/1784-1800/haitian-rev"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"US Office of the Historian · Haitian Revolution","url":"https://history.state.gov/milestones/1784-1800/haitian-rev"}]},{"id":"WHY23","region":"americas","period":"North American colonies in the 18th century","title":"Why did the American War of Independence begin?","summary":"Taxation to pay war costs developed into a dispute over colonial self-government and political representation.","causes":[{"title":"British finances","text":"After warfare, Britain sought financial contributions from the colonies."},{"title":"Self-government and representation","text":"Colonists objected to taxation while they had no representatives in Parliament, among other grievances."},{"title":"Escalating conflict","text":"Official responses to protests and resistance reinforced each other, making compromise harder."}],"trigger":"After armed clashes began, the demand for independence became clearer during the war.","chain":["Disputes over taxation and government","Political protest and armed clashes","Declaration of Independence, war and international support"],"consequences":["An independent state and new constitutional institutions emerged.","Claims of liberty coexisted with slavery and the exclusion of women, Indigenous peoples and others."],"nuance":"Independence was not initially a shared objective of all inhabitants: support, loyalty and interests were divided.","reflection":"How does explaining high taxes differ from asking who has the authority to levy them?","questions":[{"id":300044,"topic":"WHY23-0","type":"choice","era":"North American colonies in the 18th century","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY23","q":"Why did taxation become a political issue?","answer":"Representation and legitimate government mattered, not only the amount paid","options":["Representation and legitimate government mattered, not only the amount paid","British law never applied in the colonies","Every opponent of taxation wanted independence from the beginning","War costs were unrelated to British policy"],"ex":"Financial demands became linked to political participation and self-government.","refs":[{"label":"US National Archives · Road to the Declaration of Independence","url":"https://www.archives.gov/founding-docs/declaration-history"}]},{"id":300045,"topic":"WHY23-1","type":"choice","era":"North American colonies in the 18th century","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY23","q":"How should independence and rights be understood?","answer":"Distinguish ideals of liberty from the actual scope of recognized rights","options":["Distinguish ideals of liberty from the actual scope of recognized rights","The Declaration immediately abolished slavery everywhere","Everyone, including women and Indigenous people, gained equal political participation at once","There is no need to compare a new state’s ideals and institutions"],"ex":"Separate the proclamation of ideals from their scope and implementation.","refs":[{"label":"US National Archives · Road to the Declaration of Independence","url":"https://www.archives.gov/founding-docs/declaration-history"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"US National Archives · Road to the Declaration of Independence","url":"https://www.archives.gov/founding-docs/declaration-history"}]},{"id":"WHY24","region":"global","period":"The Atlantic world from the early modern period to the 19th century","title":"Why did the Atlantic slave trade expand?","summary":"Colonial labour demand was linked to violent human trafficking through state, commercial and financial institutions and racialized domination.","causes":[{"title":"Colonial demand","text":"Expanding sugar and other plantations sought large labour forces."},{"title":"Institutions and profit","text":"Merchants, investment, shipping and colonial law formed systems maintaining slavery."},{"title":"Violence and domination","text":"Capture, warfare, sale and racially discriminatory institutions deprived people of freedom."}],"trigger":"The scale of forced transportation grew alongside Atlantic commerce and expanding colonial production.","chain":["Colonial production and pursuit of profit","Trafficking and forced labour sustained by violence and institutions","Atlantic-wide economic and social transformations"],"consequences":["Vast numbers of lives and freedoms were taken, transforming societies in Africa and the Americas.","Enslaved people continued resistance and cultural transmission; their histories also connected with antislavery movements."],"nuance":"Labour demand made slavery neither inevitable nor justified, and does not remove responsibility for institutions and choices.","reflection":"Explain both the systems benefiting enslavers and the actions of people harmed by and resisting them.","questions":[{"id":300046,"topic":"WHY24-0","type":"choice","era":"The Atlantic world from the early modern period to the 19th century","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY24","q":"Which combination explains the expanding slave trade?","answer":"Labour demand, profit and institutions, and violent domination","options":["Labour demand, profit and institutions, and violent domination","Labour demand meant no alternative to slavery existed","Racial discrimination and economics were unrelated","Every African society had the same role and interests"],"ex":"Examine how demand was linked to particular institutions and violence.","refs":[{"label":"United Nations · Background to the Atlantic slave trade","url":"https://www.un.org/en/observances/decade-people-african-descent/slave-trade"}]},{"id":300047,"topic":"WHY24-1","type":"choice","era":"The Atlantic world from the early modern period to the 19th century","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY24","q":"What perspective on enslaved people is appropriate?","answer":"Consider resistance, community and cultural transmission as well as suffering","options":["Consider resistance, community and cultural transmission as well as suffering","Domination made all agency impossible","Profits cancelled out the harm","Explaining slavery is the same as justifying it"],"ex":"Recognize coercion without losing sight of participants’ own actions.","refs":[{"label":"United Nations · Background to the Atlantic slave trade","url":"https://www.un.org/en/observances/decade-people-african-descent/slave-trade"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"United Nations · Background to the Atlantic slave trade","url":"https://www.un.org/en/observances/decade-people-african-descent/slave-trade"}]},{"id":"WHY25","region":"global","period":"The world in 1914","title":"Why did the Sarajevo assassination escalate into world war?","summary":"The assassination became linked to alliances, armaments, regional rivalries and political decisions during a crisis.","causes":[{"title":"Long-term tensions","text":"National and state conflicts in the Balkans interacted with great-power competition."},{"title":"Armies and alliances","text":"Armaments, mobilization plans and alliances shaped crisis-management choices."},{"title":"Political decisions","text":"Governments chose ultimatums, support, mobilization and declarations of war, escalating the conflict."}],"trigger":"The crisis following the assassination of Austria’s heir became a wider war through successive national actions.","chain":["Regional crisis","Government choices and chains of alliance and mobilization","European war becoming global war"],"consequences":["Total war mobilized societies and economies and involved many regions, including colonies.","Empires fell, revolutions occurred and the postwar international order was reshaped."],"nuance":"Do not explain every cause through the assassination alone or assume alliances made war automatically inevitable.","reflection":"Separate long-term background, the immediate trigger and mechanisms of escalation.","questions":[{"id":300048,"topic":"WHY25-0","type":"choice","era":"The world in 1914","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY25","q":"How did the Sarajevo assassination relate to the war?","answer":"It escalated through existing tensions and government decisions","options":["It escalated through existing tensions and government decisions","The assassination automatically determined every country’s entry","Alliances eliminated every diplomatic choice","Colonies and societies beyond Europe were unrelated to the war"],"ex":"Distinguish the trigger, background and actions producing escalation.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · The Coming of War","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/11-2-the-collapse-of-the-ottomans-and-the-coming-of-war"}]},{"id":300049,"topic":"WHY25-1","type":"choice","era":"The world in 1914","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY25","q":"What should supplement an explanation based on alliances?","answer":"Examine how governments used alliances and mobilization during the crisis","options":["Examine how governments used alliances and mobilization during the crisis","Alliance texts alone make political decisions unnecessary to study","More armaments always cause war","Balkan regional circumstances become irrelevant"],"ex":"The existence of institutions differs from actions taken within them.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · The Coming of War","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/11-2-the-collapse-of-the-ottomans-and-the-coming-of-war"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · The Coming of War","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/11-2-the-collapse-of-the-ottomans-and-the-coming-of-war"}]},{"id":"WHY26","region":"global","period":"The 1920s–1930s","title":"Why did the Depression spread worldwide?","summary":"The American financial crisis affected production and daily life abroad through credit, trade and monetary systems.","causes":[{"title":"Financial connections","text":"Banks and international lending transmitted credit contraction across borders."},{"title":"Trade and demand","text":"Falling incomes and investment reduced imports, harming export-dependent regions."},{"title":"Policy and money","text":"Gold-standard constraints, protectionism and other responses affected the spread and duration of the downturn."}],"trigger":"The stock-market crash marked a turning point, but bank failures, debt and declining demand combined to deepen the crisis.","chain":["Financial and credit contraction","Declining investment, production and trade","Harm to jobs and livelihoods and political change"],"consequences":["Unemployment and social insecurity spread, and state involvement in economies changed.","Political responses differed: countries did not all necessarily adopt the same regime."],"nuance":"Distinguish the crash as an event from the mechanisms spreading and sustaining the Depression.","reflection":"How did an interconnected world economy transmit the downturn?","questions":[{"id":300050,"topic":"WHY26-0","type":"choice","era":"The 1920s–1930s","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY26","q":"What relationship explains international transmission?","answer":"Shrinking international lending and trade affected production and employment abroad","options":["Shrinking international lending and trade affected production and employment abroad","Borders prevented financial crises crossing them","The crash changed every economy in an identical way at the same instant","Protectionism always increased every trading partner’s demand"],"ex":"Consider chains operating through credit and commodity markets.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · The Great Depression","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/12-3-the-great-depression"}]},{"id":300051,"topic":"WHY26-1","type":"choice","era":"The 1920s–1930s","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY26","q":"How did the Depression relate to politics?","answer":"Countries made different political choices amid economic crisis","options":["Countries made different political choices amid economic crisis","Recession always produces dictatorship","State policy cannot affect a downturn’s course","Unemployment and social insecurity are unrelated"],"ex":"Separate changed economic conditions from political responses to them.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · The Great Depression","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/12-3-the-great-depression"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · The Great Depression","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/12-3-the-great-depression"}]},{"id":"WHY27","region":"global","period":"The 1930s–Second World War","title":"Why was the expansion of war not stopped?","summary":"Collective security and national responses did not sufficiently deter aggressive policies.","causes":[{"title":"Aggression and expansion","text":"Leaders in Nazi Germany, Japan and elsewhere pursued expansion of influence and territory by force."},{"title":"Limits of international institutions","text":"The League of Nations and other arrangements faced constraints on participation, sanctions and military action."},{"title":"Divided responses","text":"The Depression, domestic circumstances and fear of war complicated coordination and cooperation."}],"trigger":"Regional aggression and warfare expanded until conflicts in Europe and Asia-Pacific became connected.","chain":["Aggressive policies and international conflict","Failed deterrence and widening warfare","Global war and enormous destruction"],"consequences":["War brought immense harm, including many civilian deaths and genocide.","Afterwards, the UN was established and empires and the international order were reorganized."],"nuance":"Do not use weak international institutions to diminish aggressors’ responsibility. Also examine the different European and Asian sequences.","reflection":"Distinguish aggressors’ choices from the conditions faced by those attempting to stop them.","questions":[{"id":300052,"topic":"WHY27-0","type":"choice","era":"The 1930s–Second World War","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY27","q":"How should expanding warfare be explained?","answer":"Consider both decisions to invade and limits on international deterrence","options":["Consider both decisions to invade and limits on international deterrence","League weakness removed aggressors’ choices and responsibility","Every country hit by depression pursued identical aggression","European and Asian wars began through identical sequences"],"ex":"Structural conditions do not erase the responsibility of those taking action.","refs":[{"label":"United States Holocaust Memorial Museum · Context of the Second World War","url":"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-in-europe"},{"label":"OpenStax · An Unstable Peace","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/13-1-an-unstable-peace"}]},{"id":300053,"topic":"WHY27-1","type":"choice","era":"The 1930s–Second World War","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY27","q":"What matters when studying war’s expansion in Europe?","answer":"Also examine different developments in Asia and elsewhere","options":["Also examine different developments in Asia and elsewhere","One outbreak date explains the beginning in every region","Only soldiers suffered harm","Diplomatic negotiations mean no forcible expansion occurred"],"ex":"World war also involved the connection of distinct regional processes.","refs":[{"label":"United States Holocaust Memorial Museum · Context of the Second World War","url":"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-in-europe"},{"label":"OpenStax · An Unstable Peace","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/13-1-an-unstable-peace"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"United States Holocaust Memorial Museum · Context of the Second World War","url":"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-in-europe"},{"label":"OpenStax · An Unstable Peace","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/13-1-an-unstable-peace"}]},{"id":"WHY28","region":"global","period":"After the Second World War","title":"Why did the Cold War form after the war?","summary":"As wartime cooperation against Germany ended, US–Soviet security visions and political-economic systems came into sharper conflict.","causes":[{"title":"Postwar power","text":"The US and Soviet Union wielded great power while war had destabilized Europe and other regions."},{"title":"Security","text":"Each side viewed the other’s actions concerning borders and neighbouring political systems as threats."},{"title":"Ideals and institutions","text":"Policies invoking capitalism and communism became linked to reconstruction aid and spheres of influence."}],"trigger":"Disputes and policy responses over Eastern Europe, Germany and postwar recovery strengthened opposing blocs.","chain":["Distrust over the postwar settlement","Aid, political intervention and alliances","East–West rivalry and worldwide effects"],"consequences":["Military alliances and nuclear arms competition developed, while rivalry also became entangled with regional conflicts.","States and movements pursued their own aims, including non-aligned alternatives."],"nuance":"Do not explain every event as an instruction from Washington or Moscow: retain local interests and actors’ agency.","reflection":"How can actions taken against a perceived threat deepen mutual distrust?","questions":[{"id":300054,"topic":"WHY28-0","type":"choice","era":"After the Second World War","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY28","q":"Which combination explains the Cold War’s formation?","answer":"Postwar power relations, security and conflicts over systems","options":["Postwar power relations, security and conflicts over systems","Different ideals cause the same Cold War in every period","Wartime cooperation meant no postwar conflicts of interest","Politics outside the US and Soviet Union became meaningless"],"ex":"Relate concrete postwar conditions to reciprocal policies.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · The Cold War Begins","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/14-1-the-cold-war-begins"}]},{"id":300055,"topic":"WHY28-1","type":"choice","era":"After the Second World War","difficulty":"Cause and consequence","whyQuiz":true,"whyId":"WHY28","q":"How should regional Cold War histories be understood?","answer":"Consider both superpower involvement and local actors’ purposes","options":["Consider both superpower involvement and local actors’ purposes","Every regional actor followed only great-power orders","Non-aligned states were unrelated to international politics","Nuclear weapons eliminated regional wars"],"ex":"Global rivalry does not explain every aspect of regional history.","refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · The Cold War Begins","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/14-1-the-cold-war-begins"}]}],"refs":[{"label":"OpenStax · The Cold War Begins","url":"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-2/pages/14-1-the-cold-war-begins"}]}];
